WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.635
-Welcome to the show!
-Thank you.

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.871
I'm so thrilled to be here.
-You have a fascinating story.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.406
You were born in India.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.207
You became the first
Indian-American woman

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:09.575
elected to Congress.

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.444
You moved here alone,
I believe, by yourself

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:13.479
when you were 16 years old.
-I did. I did.

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.047
-And went to Georgetown.

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:18.417
What was it like being here
for the first time at that age?

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.719
-It was a strange experience.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:24.290
I was so young.
I had never seen snow before.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.293
I hated wearing socks 'cause
I had never had to do that.

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.295
-Yeah. I feel the same way
about socks.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.462
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:32.798
-I still don't like to do that.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:36.002
I had a picture of the Taj Mahal
on my wall, and --

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.803
-In your dorm?
-In my dorm.

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:39.639
Yeah, I just went out
because I was homesick

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.407
and I bought a picture
of the Taj Mahal.

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.009
It said "India" on the bottom.

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:45.078
And then my next door neighbor
came in and said,

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:47.013
"Oh, my God,
is that your home?"

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.848
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:50.582
And I thought she was kidding,
so I said,

00:00:50.582 --> 00:00:52.185
"No, it's the servants'
quarters.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:54.119
The home doesn't fit on the --"
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.119 --> 00:00:56.189
And she said, "Oh, my God,
are you a princess?"

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.124
I said, "Yes, but I'm
embarrassed to be called

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.492
Princess Pramila."

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.860
She bought the whole thing.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.528
A week later somebody came up
and said,

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:04.197
"Are you the real-life
princess?"

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.665
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.500
-Wow, how did they
get into Georgetown?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.269
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:12.572
-So you obviously,

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.441
as a co-chair of
the Progressive Caucus,

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:16.242
you've been very critical
of the President.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.743
Have you ever met the President?

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:19.579
-I really have tried not to.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.581
-[ Laughs ]
-That's probably a good...

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.316
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.819
...good idea.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:29.422
You endorsed Bernie in 2016.
-I did.

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.090
-Which makes sense as someone
who's the co-chair

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.459
of the Progressive Caucus.
You've not endorsed anybody yet.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.828
What is it that you're
looking for,

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:38.231
and are you going to endorse,
or are you going to just

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:39.832
sort of wait it out and see
what happens?

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.900
-No, I'm probably going to
endorse, but not too soon,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.171
because we have 98 members
in the Progressive Caucus.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.506
And it's a really important
thing, I think, for me

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:48.941
to just stay back
a little bit,

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:51.077
but I am looking
for somebody who is bold,

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.680
who is unafraid to take on
the special interests,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.749
who understands that
the inequality we have today

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.484
started long before Trump

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:01.421
and that we have to have
bold solutions

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.456
for some of the worst things
that we're facing --

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.792
climate crisis, inequality.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.027
Three people in the country,

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.529
two of whom live in my state,
have the same wealth

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:13.265
as the bottom
160 million people.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:17.236
So I just think we need somebody
who is willing to propose

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:20.706
solutions that match the scale
of the crisis.

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:22.709
-You --
Medicare for all...

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.544
[ Applause ]

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.445
...has been a question
in a lot of the debates.

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:27.880
They've been sort of asking
candidates,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.249
"Put your hand up if you are
pro- the idea

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:32.252
of only having
public health care,

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:33.886
no private insurance companies."
-Right.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:36.256
-You've introduced
a Medicare for all bill.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:39.792
What is it -- How do you feel
like you move this forward?

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.995
Do you believe it's what
the public wants,

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.030
and do you feel like politicians
are hearing that?

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.231
-It is absolutely
what the public wants.

00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:49.936
70% of Americans want a single
payer health care system.

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:53.606
And no, politicians are a little
bit behind the eight-ball.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.274
They're not quite there yet

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.276
because they've got to take on
the insurance companies

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.678
and the drug companies
that are profiting

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:01.113
off of people's illness.

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:02.415
That is just wrong.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.183
Everyone should have
health care.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:05.451
It should be a human right.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.220
You shouldn't have to pay
copays, premiums,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.554
and deductibles.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:10.422
And when you get sick,
you should go to the doctor.

00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:12.425
And we don't need all these
private insurance companies,

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:14.961
and they are charging
enormous amounts of money

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:17.230
and stopping people
from getting health care.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.098
-You -- Another issue
that's coming up,

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:20.600
obviously in the debates

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.270
and people are talking about it
these days is immigration.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.907
What is the value right now
of having someone like yourself

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.875
and colleagues of yours that
are in Congress right now

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:31.310
that are immigrants?

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.146
Is it -- Does it add value
in this conversation?

00:03:34.146 --> 00:03:37.216
-Yeah, absolutely.
I mean, there's 14 of us

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:39.518
out of 535 that are
actually immigrants

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:41.554
born outside
of the United States,

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.156
and it is a personal experience.

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:46.292
For me it took me 19 years
to get my citizenship,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.428
and know what
the system is like.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.096
I've worked on
immigrant rights

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:52.765
as an activist for 15 years
before coming here.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.635
And I really understand
how immigrants are often

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:58.404
the scapegoats, and that's
true for a long time,

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.439
but it's certainly true
with this President.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:03.208
And so to be able to be there
and fight back

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:05.878
and show the resilience
and the courage and the benefits

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.715
of immigrants across the country
as a member of Congress,

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:10.950
but also to be able to talk
about the policy,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.351
has been amazing.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.287
-You have -- You've only been
in Congress since 2016.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:16.489
Is that right?
-Right.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.124
-And yet there's this
freshman class right now

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:21.126
that you're sort of one of
the senior members of.

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:23.262
But I guess you can sort of
show them the ropes --

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:25.764
you know, AOC, Ilhan Omar.
-Right.

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:27.800
-And Rashida Tlaib.
-Right.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.202
-Are you a close-knit group,
as it seems?

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:31.738
-Yeah, they're amazing.

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.440
And, you know, there are
only 67 women of color

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.443
that have ever served
in Congress out of 11,000.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:40.112
And so we're a very
tight-knit group.

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:41.314
I mean, we really --

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.848
We like to make soup
with each other.

00:04:42.848 --> 00:04:44.584
We like to have wine
with each other.

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:47.954
We like to just tweet
each other out

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.888
and hold each other up.

00:04:49.888 --> 00:04:52.258
We go to each other's
press conferences.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.427
It's really wonderful.
They are smart.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:56.929
-Man, soup and wine.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.132
D.C. is as exactly as exciting
as I thought it was.

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:01.533
Hey, thank you
for making the time.

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:03.036
It was wonderful talking to you.
-Thank you.

